"reasoning": "The patient reports vomiting, diarrhea, fatigue, and abdominal pain, but the most distinctive and clinically decisive symptoms are that their skin has turned yellow and their urine is dark. These two findings are the classic hallmarks of jaundice, indicating elevated bilirubin levels typically associated with liver dysfunction or bile duct obstruction. While conditions like typhoid or malaria can cause vomiting, diarrhea, and fatigue, they do not characteristically present with yellowing of the skin and dark urine. Reference [1] in the training data describes an nearly identical symptom cluster (vomiting, tiredness, yellow skin, dark urine, abdominal pain) and correctly identifies the diagnosis as jaundice. Therefore, the facts firmly establish jaundice as the single correct diagnosis.",
"final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]jaundice[/DIAGNOSIS]"
